= State machine — Technical =

== Abstract model ==
A transition system can be represented by:

<pre>
S = set of states
I = inputs/events
T ⊆ S × I × S
</pre>

A deterministic machine constrains T so relevant state/input pairs have at most one successor.

== State abstraction ==
Choosing state is a modeling decision.

A state should preserve enough information to determine future behavior under the abstraction.

Too little state merges behaviorally different configurations; too much state can make the model unusably large.

== Extended models ==
Variants include:

* finite-state machines;
* labeled transition systems;
* statecharts;
* timed automata;
* probabilistic state machines;
* Markov models;
* hybrid automata.

They differ in assumptions about time, probability, hierarchy, concurrency and continuous variables.

== Verification ==
State-space exploration supports reachability, safety and liveness analysis, but large systems can suffer state explosion.

